@page "/devbook/fields"

<PageTitle>Metadata fields — Backlog UI Storybook</PageTitle>

<StoryPage Title="Fields"
           Summary="A subpage of Devbook: every kind of metadata on its own, drawn by the smallest component for its value. The parent page shows one block with everything in it, which is how a reader meets a record and the wrong altitude for reviewing one field — a shape that only ever renders inside the record can only be looked at by assembling a record around it. There is one component per value shape and one story per field: eighteen stories share ten shapes — a scalar, a list of plain strings, a list of addresses, a list of aliases, a list of application keys, a classification, an estimate, a GitHub reference, the extension namespace, and the keys nobody modelled — so two fields with the same shape are drawn by the same component and cannot drift apart, and three fields draw no visible label because their value already says which field it is. `status` is not here: it is the same question as state, and it has a subpage of its own — and so is the decision and review state beside it, which is never a row at all; Chapter and file draws it. Combinations are the parent page's job."
           Guidelines="@Rules">

    <Story Name="kind"
           Description="What kind of thing the entry is, out of a closed vocabulary — `format`, `runtime`, `framework`, `library`. That closedness is the fact worth drawing, so it is a classification chip rather than a value: a bare `framework` in a code span could have come from any field in the block, and an outlined chip says “this entry has been filed as one of a small number of things”.

Which is what lets the row lose its visible label. The chip says which field it came from; a dimmed `kind` in front of it was holding up something that stands on its own. The label is hidden, not dropped — the `dt` is still in the description list wearing `sr-only`, so a screen reader still hears the field name, and the tooltip carries it for a pointer user.

`.badge--kind` shares its rule with `.badge--area`, because the two are the same fact about different things: the drawer something is filed in, out of a vocabulary somebody else owns. No per-value tone, and no slug — the vocabulary belongs to the folder, and a `Slug` would put an authored word into a class name."
           Code="@KindCode">
        @Field("kind", "kind: framework", ".tech entries.", "MetadataKindBadge",
            @<MetadataKindBadge Kind="framework" />, showLabel: false)
    </Story>

    <Story Name="version"
           Description="The pinned version, as authored — and the reason the shape is a `code` element: `10.0` is a version string and not the number ten. A file writes it quoted for exactly that reason, and the reader drops the quotes and keeps the text.

The `v` is what makes it legible without a label. `v10.0` is a version anywhere it appears, which is the whole of what `Prefix` is for: the sigil is drawn inside the value rather than beside it, so a reader who has the value and no row heading still knows what they are looking at. It travels with the string a reader copies, which is the honest place for it. A prefix long enough to read as a word would be a label, and belongs in the `dt`.

Nothing here compares versions or knows what a newer one would be. The pinned version is a fact the file states, and stating it back is the whole job."
           Code="@VersionCode">
        @Field("version", "version: \"10.0\"", ".tech entries.", "MetadataScalar",
            @<MetadataScalar Value="10.0" Prefix="v" Title="version: 10.0" />, showLabel: false)
    </Story>

    <Story Name="effort"
           Description="A story-point count, drawn as a badge rather than as a value. It reads at a glance the way a status does — it is the number somebody scans a list of items for — so it wears the application's badge, in its own tone because an estimate is not a state.

The unit is spelled out, and that is also why this row needs no visible label: “5 pts” names its own field and “5” never could. The one thing a reader must not do with a story-point count is read it as days.

Zero is an estimate and draws its badge; absent draws nothing. Nobody has sized this and sized and found to be nothing are different answers.

**This field is not defined by the devbook plugin's schema, and no file in this repository writes it.** It is this repository's own extension to the schema, not plugin convention."
           Code="@EffortCode">
        @Field("effort", "effort: 5", "This repository's own addition. No folder writes it today.", "MetadataEffortBadge",
            @<MetadataEffortBadge Effort="5" />, showLabel: false)
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="devbook-fields-effort-zero">
            <p class="sb-label">An estimate of zero, which is not the same as no estimate</p>
            <dl class="devbook-fields">
                <MetadataField Label="effort" ShowLabel="false"><MetadataEffortBadge Effort="0" TestId="devbook-fields-effort-zero-badge" /></MetadataField>
            </dl>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="issue"
           Description="A GitHub issue, drawn through the family the product already draws GitHub references with. `MetadataIssueLink` renders nothing of its own: it maps the two forms a knowledge file writes onto an `IntegrationLinkRef` and hands it to `IntegrationLink`, which is where the provider mark, the label and the repository beside it come from.

Only one of the two forms can be followed. A URL is an address a browser can be handed; the `owner/repo#number` shorthand needs a remote this library knows nothing about, and guessing one would produce a link to somewhere the issue is not. So the shorthand arrives with no URL and gets the inert span `IntegrationLink`'s tri-state already has for that case — not link-coloured and not reachable by tab, because a reference nobody can follow should not invite a click or take a tab stop to refuse one.

No state chip. A `meta` fence says *which* issue, never whether it is open, and `IntegrationArtifactState.Unknown` is deliberately not used: that member means “we looked and could not tell”, and nobody looked. Inventing it would put a dashed “Not checked” chip on every chapter in the repository.

Only `http` and `https` become links. Any other scheme in a knowledge file is either a mistake or an attack, and neither is something to offer a reader a click on."
           Code="@IssueCode">
        @Field("issue", "issue: JSdotNet/Backlog#118", ".backlog items, .arc42 decisions.", "MetadataIssueLink",
            @<MetadataIssueLink Issue="JSdotNet/Backlog#118" />)
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="devbook-fields-issue-url">
            <p class="sb-label">The same issue as a URL — the same label, and now followable</p>
            <CodeView Source="issue: https://github.com/JSdotNet/Backlog/issues/118" Language="markdown" AllowCopy="false" CssClass="sb-raw__code" TestId="devbook-fields-issue-url-source" />
            <dl class="devbook-fields">
                <MetadataField Label="issue"><MetadataIssueLink Issue="https://github.com/JSdotNet/Backlog/issues/118" TestId="devbook-fields-issue-url-link" /></MetadataField>
            </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="devbook-fields-issue-malformed">
            <p class="sb-label">A value neither form parses is drawn whole — a malformed issue is a typo, and a typo is only useful once it is visible</p>
            <CodeView Source="issue: backlog-118" Language="markdown" AllowCopy="false" CssClass="sb-raw__code" TestId="devbook-fields-issue-malformed-source" />
            <dl class="devbook-fields">
                <MetadataField Label="issue"><MetadataIssueLink Issue="backlog-118" TestId="devbook-fields-issue-malformed-link" /></MetadataField>
            </dl>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="related"
           Description="The general-purpose relation, available in every folder: what this chapter points at for context, with no claim about order. Entries are addresses, which is what makes them followable — `MetadataReferenceList` loops over them and `DevbookReferenceLink` draws each one as whatever the host can actually do with it. What an address is, and the other place one is written, is Markdown → References.

Both entries resolve here, because that is what a reading surface with a route table looks like: a field of two addresses is two links. `HrefFor` is still asked once per reference rather than once per field — only the host knows which of them it can route to — and the case where it cannot is a state worth seeing, so it gets its own example below rather than one dead entry sitting in a list of two.

An entry that will not parse as an address does not vanish either — it lands under Extra, on the relation it was written on."
           Code="@ReferenceCode">
        @Field("related", "related: [\".tech/shared.md#markdown\", \".arc42/02-constraints.md\"]",
            "Every folder.", "MetadataReferenceList",
            @<MetadataReferenceList References="@RelatedReferences" HrefFor="@ResolveAll" />)
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="devbook-fields-related-unresolved">
            <p class="sb-label">The same field on a host that could not resolve either address — inert text, not a link to nowhere</p>
            <dl class="devbook-fields">
                <MetadataField Label="related" TestId="devbook-fields-related-unresolved-value"><MetadataReferenceList References="@RelatedReferences" /></MetadataField>
            </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="devbook-fields-related-action">
            <p class="sb-label">And on a host that routes in-process instead of by address: a button, doing the same job</p>
            <dl class="devbook-fields">
                <MetadataField Label="related" TestId="devbook-fields-related-action-value"><MetadataReferenceList References="@RelatedReferences" OnNavigate="@Follow" /></MetadataField>
            </dl>
            <p class="sb-state" data-testid="devbook-fields-related-followed">@Followed</p>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="depends-on"
           Description="The same value shape as `related` and a stronger statement: what has to land first. A `.tech` chapter uses it to point at the shared runtime it sits on, and a `.domain` feature uses it for a feature that has to be delivered before this one.

Nothing in the library orders anything by it. It is drawn as the addresses it holds, and what to do with the ordering is the reading surface's question."
           Code="@ReferenceCode">
        @Field("depends-on", "depends-on: [\".tech/shared.md#net-runtime\", \".tech/shared.md#c-language\"]",
            ".domain features, .backlog items, .tech entries.", "MetadataReferenceList",
            @<MetadataReferenceList References="@DependsOnReferences" HrefFor="@ResolveAll" />)
    </Story>

    <Story Name="implements (legacy)"
           Description="**Legacy.** How a `.backlog` item said what it delivers, addressed into `.domain`. `.backlog` is retiring, and devbook contract 16 no longer draws `implements` as an edge: the atlas stopped drawing it, and no current folder writes it. It is still parsed and still drawn here, because an older checkout still says it and a reader should see what the file says.

Same value shape as the other two relations, drawn by the same component. Where the addresses may point is the convention's rule, not the component's — nothing here refuses an `implements` entry that names something else."
           Code="@ReferenceCode">
        @Field("implements", "implements: [.domain/tasks/features.md#feature-roadmap-planning]",
            ".backlog items.", "MetadataReferenceList",
            @<MetadataReferenceList References="@ImplementsReferences" HrefFor="@ResolveAll" />)
    </Story>

    <Story Name="aliases"
           Description="Surface names one canonical term is also known by, each drawn as the qualifier it is. An alias applies to a *name*: `TaskItem` is not a fact about the chapter the way a status or a version is, it is another word for the thing the chapter is about. So it is set above the baseline of the text it belongs to and a step smaller than it, which is what a reader already reads as “this belongs to the word in front of it”.

The size is relative rather than a step off the type scale. A fixed one would be smaller than the body face in a domain pane and exactly the same size as everything around it inside a metadata row — superscript in one place and an ordinary chip in the other. There is a floor, because 0.8em of a 12px row is 9.6px and that is below anything else this stylesheet sets.

Followable where a host can resolve one, and only then — which is the split against `alternatives` below. The term an alias names *is* modelled somewhere, so unlike a rejected technology it is a word a host holding a naming index can place. The tri-state is `Badge`'s, newly: an address makes it an anchor, a delegate makes it a button, neither leaves it the span every other badge is. All three are below. Nothing invents a destination — an alias with no answer stays unfollowable rather than becoming a link to a page nobody wrote.

`MetadataView` wires no resolver today. What a host would resolve an alias *to* is a question no file in this repository answers, and a parameter for it on the record would be the record claiming to know."
           Code="@AliasCode">
        @Field("aliases", "aliases: [TaskItem, backlog_entry_id, backlog_item_id]",
            ".domain naming — the one file per context that names terms. 90 uses across the folder.", "MetadataAliasList",
            @<MetadataAliasList Aliases="@Aliases" />)
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="devbook-fields-aliases-linked">
            <p class="sb-label">On a host with a naming index: an anchor per alias it can place, and the one it cannot left alone</p>
            <dl class="devbook-fields">
                <MetadataField Label="aliases" TestId="devbook-fields-aliases-linked-value"><MetadataAliasList Aliases="@Aliases" HrefFor="@ResolveKnownAlias" /></MetadataField>
            </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="devbook-fields-aliases-inline">
            <p class="sb-label">Against the term it qualifies, which is where the top alignment is for</p>
            <p>The canonical term is <strong>Backlog Entry</strong><MetadataAliasList Aliases="@InlineAlias" HrefFor="@ResolveKnownAlias" />, and the badge rides above the word rather than beside it.</p>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="alternatives"
           Description="What was weighed against a technology and not adopted. Plain strings for a blunter reason than `aliases`: something that was rejected has no chapter to point at, so there is nothing an address could name and nothing a host could resolve.

`MetadataValueList` is the component that cannot make them followable — it takes no href and no navigation callback, so there is nothing here for a host to change its mind with. That is a difference in the type rather than in a parameter somebody has to remember not to pass, and it is the whole distinction between this field and the one above it.

Each entry is a `MetadataScalar`, so one string looks the same whether it arrived alone under `version` or third under `alternatives`. The gap between them is the stylesheet's, so a value copied off the screen does not bring a comma with it."
           Code="@ValueListCode">
        @Field("alternatives", "alternatives: [\"Azure Functions\", \"Controller-based ASP.NET Core\"]",
            ".tech entries.", "MetadataValueList",
            @<MetadataValueList Values="@Alternatives" />)
    </Story>

    <Story Name="feature-flag"
           Description="The key or keys of the application feature flag that delivers a `.domain` feature chapter in the running product. One chapter may name several flags, and one flag may appear on several chapters.

Drawn in the feature-badge family — `.badge--feature`, the rule `DEV` and `BETA` already wear. That family is the product's existing visual language for “an application feature is involved here”, and a flag key is the most literal case of it there is.

A key is not a status, so no slug is passed. `FeatureStatusBadge` derives its modifier from the value, which is right for a two-word vocabulary and wrong for an identifier: `feature-flag: inbox-pane` would emit `.badge--feature-inbox-pane`, a class no stylesheet defines and none ever will. The family without the per-value tone is the honest half — the shape says which family, the text says which key.

Explicitly not references: the flag lives in the application's own catalog rather than in a knowledge folder, so there is no chapter to address, no graph edge, and no key this library could validate. And an identity link rather than a status mapping — `status` says how settled the written model is, a flag's own maturity says whether the running behaviour can be relied on, and nothing here infers one from the other.

Twelve `.domain` chapters write it today. A key the reader does not model lands under Extra instead, which is the story below."
           Code="@KeyCode">
        @Field("feature-flag", "feature-flag: [inbox-pane, inbox-filters]",
            ".domain features.md Feature and Sub-feature chapters. Twelve write it today.", "MetadataKeyList",
            @<MetadataKeyList Keys="@FeatureFlags" />)
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="devbook-fields-feature-flag-single">
            <p class="sb-label">One flag, which is how most of the twelve write it</p>
            <CodeView Source="feature-flag: inbox-pane" Language="markdown" AllowCopy="false" CssClass="sb-raw__code" TestId="devbook-fields-feature-flag-single-source" />
            <dl class="devbook-fields">
                <MetadataField Label="feature-flag" TestId="devbook-fields-feature-flag-single-keys"><MetadataKeyList Keys="@SingleFeatureFlag" /></MetadataField>
            </dl>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="type"
           Description="What kind of thing a chapter or file is — `aggregate`, `library`, `context` — out of the vocabulary its folder defines: `domain/`, `tech/` and `ai/` each have one, and `arc42/` and `design/` define none. A field of the record now rather than a key it happened to keep, and drawn exactly as it was before that: a plain value under its own label.

In `.domain` the row usually is not drawn at all, because the heading above it carries the value as a mark and the same fact twice would be noise — that is the heading's page. `tech/`'s old spelling, `kind`, still reads into the same field; it keeps the classification chip above rather than being drawn a second time here, and the findings ask for the rename."
           Code="@TypeCode">
        @Field("type", "type: format", "domain/, tech/, ai/. A warning in arc42/ and design/, which define none.", "MetadataValueList",
            @<MetadataValueList Values="@TypeValue" />)
    </Story>

    <Story Name="tests"
           Description="The test cases that assert what the chapter claims, each a `<level>:<runner>:<selector>` identifier. The two below are the rule's own example.

Plain strings in the value face, one code span per entry, and never links: an entry is something a runner can be handed, not an address in the repository, and it produces no graph edge. Only the first two colons delimit — a pytest node id or a `file:line` carries colons of its own — so nothing here splits an entry further; the whole string is what a reader copies into a terminal."
           Code="@ValueListCode">
        @Field("tests", "tests: [unit:dotnet:Ordering.Domain.Tests.OrderTests, e2e:playwright:tests/checkout.spec.ts#Guest checkout completes]",
            "Every folder, chapter and file level.", "MetadataValueList",
            @<MetadataValueList Values="@TestEntries" />)
    </Story>

    <Story Name="number"
           Description="This document's place in its directory — arc42 chapter 9, TDR 2 — for the file whose name does not already say it. File-level only: a chapter's place is its place in the document, and the findings say so when a chapter writes one.

A scalar, because it is a count and not a label; the row keeps its name because a bare `9` could be anything."
           Code="@ScalarCode">
        @Field("number", "number: 9", "File-level blocks, any folder. Rare: a numbered filename supplies it.", "MetadataScalar",
            @<MetadataScalar Value="9" />)
    </Story>

    <Story Name="index"
           Description="How a document steers the generated outline: `root` makes it its directory's entry point, `exclude` keeps it out of the reading outline while leaving it in the graph. File-level only, and one of those two words — anything else is reported rather than silently ignored."
           Code="@ScalarCode">
        @Field("index", "index: root", "File-level blocks — a context's context.md declares root.", "MetadataScalar",
            @<MetadataScalar Value="root" />)
    </Story>

    <Story Name="date"
           Description="The day the chapter records — when a decision was taken, when debt was logged. Part of the content, and deliberately never a last-modified stamp: git already knows that, and a hand-kept one goes stale the first time someone forgets. Drawn as authored."
           Code="@ScalarCode">
        @Field("date", "date: 2026-09-17", "Every folder, where the date is part of the content.", "MetadataScalar",
            @<MetadataScalar Value="2026-09-17" />)
    </Story>

    <Story Name="deployment"
           Description="How a bounded context ships: `service`, a deployable of its own, or `module`, inside a modular monolith beside other contexts. Written on the context map's `bounded-context` chapter and on that context's `context.md` file block, and nowhere else — an aggregate or a feature ships with its context, which is what the findings say to a chapter that writes one."
           Code="@ScalarCode">
        @Field("deployment", "deployment: module", "domain/: a bounded-context chapter, and context.md's file block.", "MetadataScalar",
            @<MetadataScalar Value="module" />)
    </Story>

    <Story Name="ext"
           Description="The extension namespace: state a plugin layered on top of devbook keeps about a chapter, written `ext.<plugin>.<key>`. The rule's own example is the first line below.

One row, not one per key. These belong to somebody else — the convention carries them untouched, validates nothing, and forbids reading another plugin's keys as schema — so given a row each they would sit among the fields as though they were ours. Gathered under one `ext` label they read as what they are: present, verbatim, and visibly not devbook's. Shown rather than folded, because there are rarely more than a few and a hidden value is the invisible-field problem the record exists to avoid.

The key keeps the author's casing, the value is exactly what follows the colon, and an empty value is kept and drawn as the key and its colon: omit-when-empty is one of the rules this namespace is exempt from. The tooltip carries the full key to search the fence for."
           Code="@ExtCode">
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="devbook-fields-ext">
            <p class="sb-label">The lines an author writes</p>
            <CodeView Source="@ExtBlock" Language="markdown" AllowCopy="false" CssClass="sb-raw__code" TestId="devbook-fields-ext-source" />
            <p class="sb-state">Every folder. Owned by whichever plugin wrote it; never validated here.</p>
            <p class="sb-state">Drawn by <code>MetadataExtensionList</code> over what that block parsed to, inside the one row the record gives it</p>
            <dl class="devbook-fields">
                <MetadataField Label="ext" TestId="devbook-fields-ext-value"><MetadataExtensionList Ext="@ExtMetadata.Ext" /></MetadataField>
            </dl>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="Keys the schema does not define"
           Description="The convention says not to invent fields, and this is what happens when one is invented anyway — or, far more usefully, when the schema genuinely grows and this library has not caught up. A reader that dropped an unknown key would make that addition invisible: the field in the file, absent from the view, and nobody able to say which of the two was wrong.

`MetadataExtraFields` draws rows rather than a value cell, because how many rows there are is a property of the file rather than of the schema and each label is a key nobody knew about. The values are plain strings and stay plain strings: nothing here knows what an unrecognised key means, so nothing here may promise a destination for it.

It is also where a `related` entry that will not parse as an address lands, under the relation it was written on — the second row below.

A key the reader recognises and deliberately drops never arrives here. `order` is the one of those: it is read, recognised and dropped, because a directory's reading order is not metadata about the chapter whose fence it happens to share. Leaving it out of the reader's known-field set would not remove it, it would move it into this list."
           Code="@ExtraCode">
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="devbook-fields-extra">
            <p class="sb-label">The lines an author writes</p>
            <CodeView Source="@ExtraBlock" Language="markdown" AllowCopy="false" CssClass="sb-raw__code" TestId="devbook-fields-extra-source" />
            <p class="sb-state">No folder. Anything the reader does not recognise, under whatever name it was written.</p>
            <p class="sb-state">Drawn by <code>MetadataExtraFields</code> over what that block parsed to</p>
            <dl class="devbook-fields">
                <MetadataExtraFields Extra="@ExtraMetadata.Extra" />
            </dl>
            <p class="sb-state" data-testid="devbook-fields-extra-state">@ExtraSummary</p>
        </div>
    </Story>

</StoryPage>

@code {
    private static readonly DesignGuideline[] Rules =
    [
        new("content-editing.md#structured-metadata-sigils", "The fenced metadata grammar these fields are written in."),
        new("typography-and-layout.md#metadata-lines", "How a field and its value sit above the prose they describe."),
        new("color-scheme.md#badge-and-chip-tones", "The tone a classification, an estimate, an alias and a flag key are drawn in.")
    ];

    /// <summary>
    /// The frame every story on this page shares: the line an author writes, which
    /// folders write it, the component that draws the value, and the value inside
    /// the row component the record wraps it in.
    ///
    /// <para>A helper rather than a copy per field, for the reason the components
    /// themselves exist: the frame was the same four elements every time and only
    /// the value differed, so a copy per field was a chance per field to label one of
    /// them wrongly. What is not shared is the prose — that is the story's, because
    /// what is worth saying about a field is not a template.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><paramref name="showLabel"/> is passed through rather than inferred from
    /// the field name, so a story showing a labelless row is showing the same
    /// parameter the record sets and not a second way of hiding a label.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment Field(
        string label,
        string line,
        string folders,
        string component,
        RenderFragment value,
        bool showLabel = true) =>
        @<div class="sb-stack" data-testid="@($"devbook-fields-{label}")">
            <p class="sb-label">The line an author writes</p>
            <CodeView Source="@line" Language="markdown" AllowCopy="false" CssClass="sb-raw__code"
                      TestId="@($"devbook-fields-{label}-source")" />
            <p class="sb-state">@folders</p>
            @* `sb-label` uppercases, and a component name put through that comes
               out as KNOWLEDGEKEYLIST — the word boundaries a reader needs to
               recognise the type are exactly what the transform destroys. So the
               name is a code span in a state line instead, which is also what it
               is: a fact about the rendering rather than a heading over it. *@
            <p class="sb-state">Drawn by <code>@component</code>, inside the row the record wraps every field in@(showLabel ? "" : " — with the label hidden, not dropped")</p>
            <dl class="devbook-fields">
                <MetadataField Label="@label" ShowLabel="@showLabel" TestId="@($"devbook-fields-{label}-value")">@value</MetadataField>
            </dl>
        </div>;

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<DevbookReference> RelatedReferences =
    [
        DevbookReference.Parse(".tech/shared.md#markdown")!,
        DevbookReference.Parse(".arc42/02-constraints.md")!
    ];

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<DevbookReference> DependsOnReferences =
    [
        DevbookReference.Parse(".tech/shared.md#net-runtime")!,
        DevbookReference.Parse(".tech/shared.md#c-language")!
    ];

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<DevbookReference> ImplementsReferences =
        [DevbookReference.Parse(".domain/tasks/features.md#feature-roadmap-planning")!];

    /// <summary>Answers for every reference it is asked about, which is what a
    /// reading surface with a route table does. The earlier version of this resolved
    /// `.tech` and nothing else so one story could show a link and an inert entry
    /// side by side — it read as a bug rather than as a demonstration, because a
    /// reader has no way to tell a deliberately unanswered address from a broken
    /// one. The unresolved state has its own example under `related` instead.</summary>
    private static string? ResolveAll(DevbookReference reference) => "#devbook-fields-target";

    /// <summary>Answers for the alias a naming index would hold and not for the two
    /// column names beside it, which is the honest shape of this resolver: the claim
    /// is about the individual word. One alias may be the canonical term's own class
    /// name and the next a legacy database column, and a host that can place one and
    /// not the other tells the truth by saying so.</summary>
    private static string? ResolveKnownAlias(string alias) =>
        alias is "TaskItem" ? "#devbook-fields-target" : null;

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<string> Aliases = ["TaskItem", "backlog_entry_id", "backlog_item_id"];

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<string> InlineAlias = ["TaskItem"];

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<string> Alternatives = ["Azure Functions", "Controller-based ASP.NET Core"];

    /// <summary>Both flags are real: they are the keys `.domain/capture` and
    /// `.domain/tasks` chapters name, so the story is showing a line this
    /// repository contains rather than filler.</summary>
    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<string> FeatureFlags = ["inbox-pane", "inbox-filters"];

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<string> SingleFeatureFlag = ["inbox-pane"];

    private DevbookReference? _followed;

    private string Followed => _followed is null
        ? "Nothing followed yet. Press one of the buttons above."
        : $"OnNavigate last reported: {_followed.Raw}.";

    private void Follow(DevbookReference reference) => _followed = reference;

    /// <summary>Two unrecognised keys of the two kinds that reach Extra: one the
    /// schema has never heard of, and one entry of a field it does define that will
    /// not parse as an address.</summary>
    private const string ExtraBlock = """
        status: adopted
        owner: platform-team
        related: [not an address]
        """;

    private static readonly MetadataRecord ExtraMetadata = MetadataReader.Parse(ExtraBlock);

    /// <summary>Counted off the parsed record rather than off the block, so the
    /// claim is the reader's answer and not a restatement of the source above it.
    /// The `order` line is parsed here too, and states nothing.</summary>
    private static string ExtraSummary =>
        $"Kept under Extra: {string.Join(", ", ExtraMetadata.Extra.Keys)}. "
        + $"{ExtraMetadata.References.Count} references parsed from that related field. "
        + $"The same block with order: [overview.md] added keeps "
        + $"{MetadataReader.Parse(ExtraBlock + "\norder: [overview.md]").Extra.Count} Extra keys — order is "
        + "recognised and dropped, not collected here.";

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<string> TypeValue = ["format"];

    /// <summary>The rule's own <c>tests</c> example, entry for entry.</summary>
    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<string> TestEntries =
    [
        "unit:dotnet:Ordering.Domain.Tests.OrderTests",
        "e2e:playwright:tests/checkout.spec.ts#Guest checkout completes"
    ];

    /// <summary>The rule's extension example, and two keys that show what "verbatim"
    /// covers: a plugin's own casing, and a key written with no value.</summary>
    private const string ExtBlock = """
        ext.some-plugin.checked: 2026-09-17
        ext.Review-Bot.lastRun: "2026-09-20 08:00"
        ext.some-plugin.seen:
        """;

    private static readonly MetadataRecord ExtMetadata = MetadataReader.Parse(ExtBlock);

    private const string TypeCode = """
        @* A field of the record, drawn as it always was: a plain value under its
           own label. A value read from the legacy `kind` is the kind chip instead,
           so the same fact is never drawn under two names. *@
        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Metadata.Type) && !Metadata.TypeReadFromKind)
        {
            <MetadataField Label="type"><MetadataValueList Values="@(new[] { Metadata.Type! })" /></MetadataField>
        }
        """;

    private const string ScalarCode = """
        @* One authored value in the value face. The row keeps its label: unlike a
           version or an estimate, a bare `9`, `root` or `module` does not say which
           field it came from. *@
        <MetadataField Label="deployment"><MetadataScalar Value="@Metadata.Deployment" /></MetadataField>
        """;

    private const string ExtCode = """
        @* One row for the whole namespace. Keys arrive without their `ext.`, in
           the author's casing; values exactly as written, empty ones included. *@
        <MetadataField Label="ext"><MetadataExtensionList Ext="@Metadata.Ext" /></MetadataField>

        @* Inside: key and value as one scalar, the full key on the tooltip. *@
        <MetadataScalar Value="@Entry(key, value)" Title="@($"ext.{key}")" />
        """;

    private const string KindCode = """
        @* A closed vocabulary drawn as a classification. No Slug: the vocabulary
           belongs to the folder, and an authored word in a class name is how
           `.badge--kind-framework` becomes a rule nobody wrote. *@
        <MetadataKindBadge Kind="@metadata.Kind" />

        @* The row hides the name rather than dropping it. The chip says which
           field it is to somebody looking, and nothing at all to somebody
           listening, so the <dt> stays and wears sr-only. *@
        <MetadataField Label="kind" ShowLabel="false">
        """;

    private const string VersionCode = """
        @* The prefix is drawn inside the value, so it travels with the string a
           reader copies. Blank in, nothing out — a prefix alone is not a value. *@
        <MetadataScalar Value="@metadata.Version" Prefix="v" Title="version" />
        """;

    private const string EffortCode = """
        @* Null draws nothing; 0 draws its badge. The row's own presence is the
           record's question, because it is the row that must not appear. *@
        <MetadataEffortBadge Effort="@metadata.Effort" />

        @* The unit is the component's, not the caller's: "5" on its own does not
           say what it counts — which is also why the row needs no label. *@
        <Badge Kind="effort" Title="effort: 5 story points">@effort pts</Badge>
        """;

    private const string IssueCode = """
        @* No markup of its own. The two authored forms become the reference
           record the integrations family takes, and IntegrationLink draws it. *@
        <MetadataIssueLink Issue="@metadata.Issue" />

        @* Inside: no Url means IntegrationLink's inert span, which is exactly
           what the shorthand is. No state either — a fence says which issue,
           never whether it is open. *@
        new IntegrationLinkRef(
            Id: raw,
            Provider: IntegrationProvider.GitHub,
            Kind: IntegrationLinkKind.Issue,
            Label: $"#{number}",
            Url: IsFollowable(raw) ? raw : null,
            Repository: new IntegrationRepositoryRef(repository, repository));
        """;

    private const string ReferenceCode = """
        @* One field's addresses, offered the same way as each other. HrefFor is
           asked per reference because only the host knows which it can route
           to; returning null leaves that one inert beside its siblings. *@
        <MetadataReferenceList References="@metadata.DependsOn"
                                HrefFor="@ResolveHref"
                                OnNavigate="@Follow" />

        @* What each entry becomes is still the link component's answer: an href
           makes it a link, a handler makes it a button, neither leaves it text. *@
        <DevbookReferenceLink Reference="@reference" Href="@href" OnNavigate="@Follow" />
        """;

    private const string AliasCode = """
        @* Asked per alias, because the claim is about the individual word: one
           may be the canonical term's class name and the next a legacy column. *@
        <MetadataAliasList Aliases="@metadata.Aliases" HrefFor="@ResolveAlias" />

        @* The tri-state is Badge's now. An empty EventCallback reports no
           delegate, which is what Badge reads to decide against a button — so an
           unwired list renders spans rather than controls that refuse. *@
        <Badge Kind="alias" Href="@HrefFor?.Invoke(alias)" OnClick="@Select(alias)">@alias</Badge>
        """;

    private const string ValueListCode = """
        @* Plain strings that stay plain strings. The guarantee is the type: this
           component takes no href and no navigation callback, so there is
           nothing here for a host to make followable. *@
        <MetadataValueList Values="@metadata.Alternatives" />
        """;

    private const string KeyCode = """
        @* Keys something outside these files answers to, drawn in the feature
           badge family. Nothing is clickable: filtering by a key needs a list to
           filter, and a badge that took focus to do nothing would be worse than
           a label. *@
        <MetadataKeyList Keys="@metadata.FeatureFlag" />

        @* No Slug, and that is the whole of it. FeatureStatusBadge derives its
           modifier from the value, which would emit .badge--feature-inbox-pane —
           a class no stylesheet defines. *@
        <Badge Kind="feature">@key</Badge>
        """;

    private const string ExtraCode = """
        @* Every key the reader did not recognise, under the name it was written.
           Rows rather than a value cell: how many there are is a property of the
           file, not of the schema. *@
        <MetadataExtraFields Extra="@metadata.Extra" />

        @* A key the reader recognises and deliberately drops never gets here.
           MetadataReader.KnownFields is why `order` is one of those. *@
        foreach (var (key, values) in fields)
        {
            if (KnownFields.Contains(key)) continue;
            extra[key] = values;
        }
        """;
}
